from mk import mk
mk("Mm5eO24aAec","3 Stereo Width Tricks (That Sound Great In Mono)",
 "short-ir-widener-width-trim-drawn-pan-multiband-opposite-haas",
 "Mono-safe width tricks: a 100%-wet short room IR as a stealth widener with width trim and gain make-up, a drawn and smoothed auto-pan rhythm, and Haas kept ≤15 ms split across bands tilted in opposite directions",
 "Widen one headline element so it still works in mono. (1) Stealth IR widener: convolution with a short, thin-room impulse at 100% wet and decay down to ~20% so there is no audible tail. It widens and re-colours the chords. Because width makes the part quieter in mono, trim stereo width to ~65% and add ~+6.5 dB to match levels instead of lowering the mix. (2) Rhythmic auto-pan: start ~50% amount, then draw a stepped pan curve that fits the arp, round it with smoothing, optionally switch to unsynced Hz set by ear, and add a touch of multiband treble reverb before the panner. (3) Haas without the lopsided, phasey mono sum: keep the L/R delay ≤15 ms, tune it while listening in mono, tilt a mid band to one side and the treble band to the other, then reduce width and re-level. Use it on incidental FX rather than lead vocals. Use width with restraint so the one wide element stands out. Delta vs our Haas/phase-offset, high-band pan and asymmetric L/R skills: the IR widener with width trim, drawn pan rhythm, and opposite-tilt multiband Haas.",
 ["Convolution reverb with short room IRs (Web Audio: ConvolverNode with a short stereo IR, implementation note)","Stereo width control and gain","Auto-pan / drawable pan LFO; multiband splitter; short L/R delay","Mono-sum monitoring"],
 [("source","audio","Synth chords, arp or incidental FX to widen",True)],
 [("wide_mono_safe_part","audio","Wider element that keeps level and solidity in mono")],
 ["Always check in mono: width makes a part relatively quieter in mono, so trim width and make up gain.","Haas >~40 ms is heard as a separate echo; <~40 ms fuses into width, and ≤15 ms reduces mono phasing.","Use one standout wide element; if everything is wide, nothing feels wide.","Source is a vendor tutorial; techniques are mapped to generic devices."],
 [("Insert convolution reverb on the chords at 100% wet with a short, thin room IR and decay reduced to ~20% so no tail is heard. Audition other short IRs for different timbres.","ir widener","Chords sound similar but wider and re-coloured, not reverberant"),
  ("Sum to mono: the widened chords drop in level. Instead of lowering the wet mix, follow with a width control at ~65% and raise gain ~6.5 dB to level-match.","width trim","Wide in stereo, balanced in mono, wrapping around a mono bass"),
  ("Rhythmic pan for arps/synths: auto-pan at ~50% amount, try LFO shapes, then draw a custom stepped pan curve and add smoothing to round its edges. Optionally switch rate to unsynced Hz and set by ear to fit the part.","pan rhythm","Part bounces between speakers yet stays centred in mono"),
  ("Add a touch of multiband treble-only reverb before the panner for air; adjust pan mix lower for just a flutter, or full for strong L/R jumps.","air","Airy top end without washing the lows"),
  ("Haas: duplicate L/R with a delay on one side. Keep it at ≤15 ms and tune it while listening in mono until acceptable.","haas","Ultra-wide with reduced mono phasing"),
  ("Fix the lopsided tilt: split into bands, tilt the mid band to the right and the treble band to the left, then reduce width and re-level.","multiband haas","Wide, balanced image that is acceptable in mono"),
  ("Apply these to one headline sound (incidental FX/ear candy for Haas) while keeping most of the mix centred.","restraint","Contrast makes the wide element pop in mono and stereo")],
 ["Mono sum of the IR-widened chords is level-matched to bypass within ~1 dB.","Auto-panned arp remains audible and solid in mono.","Haas delay is ≤15 ms; the image no longer leans to one side after the multiband tilt.","Only one or two elements in the mix use these width tricks."],
 ["stereo-width","mono-compatible","convolution","auto-pan","haas","multiband","mixing","web-audio"],
 note="Vendor-sponsored tutorial; plugin names mapped to generic convolution/width/pan modules; demo links dropped.")

mk("Futy449F9OY","The Secret to HUGE Dark Vocal Pads in Serum 2",
 "dark-hybrid-choir-pad-natural-highs-synthetic-lows-notch-ramp-fx",
 "Dark hybrid vocal pad: choir-like wavetable plus an octave-up choir sample FM'd in (~34%), with sub and a little noise. Lows/mids are low-passed while the natural sample keeps the highs, a subtle LFO notch moves the mids, and one slow rising ramp drives the FX",
 "Build a dark, haunting vocal pad for intros, breakdowns or tension layers by blending a real choir sample with synthetic layers that sound vocal. Osc A: a choir-type wavetable with chorus-like unison and its position moved by a single slow, rising ramp (one main movement). Osc B: a choir multisample an octave up with light tube saturation, frequency-modulating A at ~34% for grit. Add a default sub (choir has little sub) and very low white noise (or hum) for full-spectrum air. Route A + sub through a low-pass, but keep B (natural) out of it so the high end comes from the real voice. Then all go into filter 2, a subtle notch (~18%) whose amount and cutoff move on the LFO for mid-range expression. FX: a blended, very wide frequency-shifter/blur stereo layer (mix ~half), dimension ~6%, hyper-chorus automated up to ~32% at the top of the ramp, ping-pong delay ~10–11%, distortion only above ~300 Hz, a little comb, reverb ~10%, then a high-end presence boost. Delta vs our pad skills: natural-highs / synthetic-lows routing and the single-ramp FX bloom on a vocal pad.",
 ["Wavetable synth with a choir-like table and a sample/multisample oscillator that can FM osc A (Web Audio: AudioBufferSource choir + oscillator with FM via GainNode to frequency, implementation note)","Sub and noise oscillators, two filters incl. notch","FX: frequency-shift/blur widener, chorus/dimension, ping-pong delay, high-passed distortion, comb, reverb, EQ"],
 [("choir_sample","audio","Original/licensed choir 'ah'/'oo' recording (no vendor factory content)",True),("chords","midi","Long held chords",True)],
 [("dark_vocal_pad","audio","Cohesive dark choir pad with natural highs and synthetic low/mid power")],
 ["Use your own or licensed choir material; vendor wavetable/multisample names are omitted.","Most FX are very subtle (≈6–11% mixes); the character comes from stacking many small moves.","Distortion is applied above ~300 Hz only; full-band saturation gets muddy.","Percentages are the presenter's knob values; treat as starting points."],
 [("Osc A: choir-like wavetable, unison for chorus, slight low-pass. Create one main modulator, a slow triangle that effectively ramps up, on wavetable position (and later FX amounts).","osc A","Slowly evolving choir tone"),
  ("Osc B: choir multisample an octave higher with a bit of tube saturation, defaults otherwise. FM it into osc A at ~34% for a touch of grit.","osc B + FM","Gritty, breathy vocal blend"),
  ("Add a sub oscillator at default level under everything, and white noise (or hum) at a very low level for full-spectrum air.","sub + noise","Low-end weight and subtle high air"),
  ("Filter 1: low-pass on osc A + sub only; route osc B around it so the natural choir provides the high end.","routing","Synthetic layers give low/mid power; natural sample keeps highs"),
  ("Filter 2 on the combined signal: notch at ~18% mix (100% shows the full effect), with the amount rising on the LFO and the cutoff moving slightly too, for mid-range expression and cohesion.","notch","Subtle moving mid-range"),
  ("FX 1: a frequency-shifter/blur stereo layer, mix ~half, blur ~half, shift and range lowered, blended in as a moving super-wide layer. Dimension ~6%. Hyper-chorus automated from low up to ~32% at the top of the ramp.","width","Wide moving layer that turns phasey only at the peak"),
  ("FX 2: ping-pong delay ~10–11%; distortion with a high-pass so only ~300 Hz upward is saturated; a little comb filter (macro candidate); reverb ~10%; final high-shelf presence boost.","finish","Cohesive, polished, dark choir pad")],
 ["Muting osc B removes most of the natural high end.","Notch at 18% is subtle; sweeping to 100% reveals the full notch.","Distortion leaves content below ~300 Hz clean.","Over a held chord, chorus/phasing increases toward the end of the ramp."],
 ["vocal-pad","choir","hybrid","fm","notch","pad","dark","web-audio"],
 note="Preset walkthrough; factory content names omitted; pack promo dropped.")
